Mark Zuckerberg is building a new surveillance state

by John Mac Ghlionn, opinion contributor - 05/10/25 1:00 PM ET










Mark Zuckerberg recently took to Instagram to boast that nearly a billion people now use Meta AI across the company‚Äôs platforms. To celebrate, he announced the launch of a new standalone app, encouraging users to ‚ÄúCheck it out!‚Äù It sounded innocuous, almost charming, as if he were discussing a playful new feature. But make no mistake: this wasn‚Äôt just a product release. It was a warning shot from a man who now has his hands firmly on the wheel of reality.

The new app is only part of the story. The real revolution ‚Äî and the real threat ‚Äî lies in what comes next: Meta‚Äôs AI glasses. Sunglasses, spectacles, whatever you want to call them ‚Äî they look like something out of a sci-fi flick. But they‚Äôre real, and they‚Äôre here. Very soon, millions or perhaps tens of millions of people will be walking around with them on. And you might not even know it.


These aren‚Äôt just toys. They‚Äôre tools ‚Äî and weapons. They comprise a camera, microphone, an AI interface and internet access, all embedded discreetly in eyewear. They are capable of recognizing faces, interpreting language, overlaying information in real-time and collecting vast swaths of data as their owners simply walk down the street. They can whisper comprehensive summaries about the stranger across the subway, translate foreign speech in real time, suggest pickup lines, record interactions without consent and overlay reviews of a restaurant before you‚Äôve even looked at the menu.

All this is done without lifting a phone or typing a word. These glasses are not just watching the world. They are interpreting, filtering and rewriting it with the full force of Meta‚Äôs algorithms behind the lens. And if you think you‚Äôre safe just because you‚Äôre not wearing a pair, think again, because the people who wear them will inevitably point them in your direction.

You will be captured, analyzed and logged, whether you like it or not.

Every sidewalk encounter becomes a data point. Every offhand comment, facial expression, or glance across the room becomes part of the feed. And you won‚Äôt be able to opt out. These glasses will not only collect data but also send it back to Meta‚Äôs servers to be processed, monetized and repurposed: facial recognition, behavioral prediction, sentiment analysis ‚Äî all happening in real time. The implications are staggering. This isn‚Äôt just about surveillance. It‚Äôs about control of perception.

Meta‚Äôs glasses will create a world of layered realities where truth is fluid, curated and mediated by algorithm. And the algorithm, of course, is written by Meta. Imagine a future where you‚Äôre at a job interview. The person across the table is wearing Meta glasses. They can see a summary of your public online activity. They know your political leanings, your social network connections, your digital footprint. You don‚Äôt even know what they know, but it‚Äôs shaping how they see you.

Or imagine walking down a street and having your face scanned by a dozen pairs of AI glasses, your expressions analyzed, your emotional state cataloged by strangers in real time.


You didn‚Äôt sign up for this, but your image becomes fair game. Now scale it up. Multiply that by tens of millions. A society where every interaction becomes a transaction. Every human moment, an opportunity for data extraction. Every unguarded second, a potential violation. Meta doesn‚Äôt just want your attention anymore. It wants your environment, your context, your reality.

Mark Zuckerberg isn‚Äôt building a product; he‚Äôs building a world ‚Äî a filtered, augmented, monitored and monetized world where Meta AI becomes the interface between your mind and your surroundings. Forget the phone in your pocket. That was the last battlefield. The war now moves to your face. With the AI app integrated seamlessly into the glasses, users can ask questions aloud, receive contextual answers about anything they‚Äôre looking at, and even record what they‚Äôre seeing ‚Äî automatically transcribed and stored.

Meta AI becomes your co-pilot, pumping curated truths directly into your ear. And if history has shown us anything, it‚Äôs that these truths won‚Äôt be neutral. They‚Äôll be shaped by ideology, politics and profit ‚Äî the forces that already twist what we see on Instagram and Facebook. Zuckerberg likes to say Meta is about connecting people. But in reality, Meta is about framing people. It‚Äôs about shaping what they see, interpreting others and moving through the world.

It‚Äôs a shift as profound as Johannes Gutenberg‚Äôs printing press or the invention of television, but far more personal, invasive and opaque. And for those who think this sounds like hyperbole, remember that Meta already controls platforms used by billions ‚Äî most notably Instagram and Facebook. Integration of glasses into this ecosystem won‚Äôt require mandates. It will happen through incentives ‚Äî enhanced features, exclusive experiences and augmented perks for users who wear them. And before long, opting in will feel like opting out of modern life. This is how normalization begins ‚Äî not with force, but with frictionless adoption.

While the press discusses Apple‚Äôs bulky VR headsets and TikTok controversies, few grasp the totality of Meta‚Äôs vision. This isn‚Äôt just about hardware or software; it is about owning the infrastructure of reality itself. We‚Äôre witnessing the privatization of sight, the commercialization of perception, and the algorithmic colonization of daily life.

And with nearly half the world‚Äôs population already wired into the Meta ecosystem, who‚Äôs left to stop Zuckerberg from turning the rest of reality into a profitable product? Governments are asleep at the wheel, regulators are toothless and users ‚Äî seduced by convenience, novelty and social validation ‚Äî are already lining up to become beta testers in this new augmented society. The truth isn‚Äôt hidden; it‚Äôs staring us in the face.


Zuckerberg doesn‚Äôt need to control what you think, just what you see. Soon, thanks to a sleek pair of glasses and a few billion lines of code, he might control both.

John Mac Ghlionn is a writer and researcher who explores culture, society and the impact of technology on daily life.</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-2" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="308a57a9-6093-49ee-8777-31e90c6957a6" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="253" class="">This opinion piece by John Mac Ghlionn presents a stark and cautionary view of Meta's new AI glasses, framing them as a gateway to a pervasive surveillance state under the guise of innovation and convenience. The key points can be summarized as follows:</p>
<h3 data-start="255" data-end="276" class=""><strong data-start="259" data-end="276">Main Argument</strong></h3>
<p data-start="277" data-end="600" class="">Meta‚Äôs rollout of AI-enabled glasses is not merely a technological advance‚Äîit is a fundamental reengineering of public life, perception, and interpersonal interaction. These glasses allow for real-time data capture and algorithmic interpretation of the world, often without the consent or awareness of those being observed.</p>
<hr data-start="602" data-end="605" class="">
<h3 data-start="607" data-end="625" class=""><strong data-start="611" data-end="625">Key Claims</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="626" data-end="1416">
<li data-start="626" data-end="754" class="">
<p data-start="628" data-end="754" class=""><strong data-start="628" data-end="653">Surveillance by Proxy</strong>: Even non-users will be involuntarily scanned, analyzed, and recorded by people wearing the glasses.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="755" data-end="907" class="">
<p data-start="757" data-end="907" class=""><strong data-start="757" data-end="796">Invisible Infrastructure of Control</strong>: Meta is constructing a reality-filtering apparatus where user perception is mediated by corporate algorithms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="908" data-end="1063" class="">
<p data-start="910" data-end="1063" class=""><strong data-start="910" data-end="951">Normalization through Incentivization</strong>: Adoption won‚Äôt require coercion; it will be made irresistible through perks and integration into digital life.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1064" data-end="1227" class="">
<p data-start="1066" data-end="1227" class=""><strong data-start="1066" data-end="1092">Perception as Property</strong>: Meta aims to monopolize not just attention, but context‚Äîtransforming the visual and social environment into proprietary data streams.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1228" data-end="1416" class="">
<p data-start="1230" data-end="1416" class=""><strong data-start="1230" data-end="1253">Ideological Shaping</strong>: ‚ÄúCurated truths‚Äù provided by Meta AI risk reinforcing biases already present in its platforms, further entrenching a politicized and commercialized epistemology.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1418" data-end="1421" class="">
<h3 data-start="1423" data-end="1450" class=""><strong data-start="1427" data-end="1450">Underlying Concerns</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1451" data-end="1863">
<li data-start="1451" data-end="1563" class="">
<p data-start="1453" data-end="1563" class=""><strong data-start="1453" data-end="1472">Consent Erosion</strong>: No practical way to opt out of being scanned or interpreted by AI glasses worn by others.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1564" data-end="1682" class="">
<p data-start="1566" data-end="1682" class=""><strong data-start="1566" data-end="1589">Regulatory Lethargy</strong>: Governments and oversight bodies lag behind in responding to the socio-technological shift.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1683" data-end="1863" class="">
<p data-start="1685" data-end="1863" class=""><strong data-start="1685" data-end="1706">Epistemic Capture</strong>: Control over ‚Äúwhat is seen‚Äù may evolve into control over what is <em data-start="1773" data-end="1783">believed</em>, echoing broader concerns about the manipulation of public discourse and truth.</p>
</li>
