<hr data-start="248" data-end="251" class="">
<h2 data-start="253" data-end="298" class=""><strong data-start="256" data-end="298">Schema-First Interface Protocol (SFIP)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="300" data-end="318" class=""><strong data-start="304" data-end="318">I. Purpose</strong></h3>
<p data-start="319" data-end="375" class="">To create a digital and conceptual interface layer that:</p>
<ul data-start="376" data-end="624">
<li data-start="376" data-end="437" class="">
<p data-start="378" data-end="437" class="">Preserves <em data-start="388" data-end="401">compression</em>, <em data-start="403" data-end="412">clarity</em>, and <em data-start="418" data-end="437">structural intent</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="438" data-end="506" class="">
<p data-start="440" data-end="506" class="">Rejects automatic transformation into performative narrative forms</p>
</li>
<li data-start="507" data-end="624" class="">
<p data-start="509" data-end="624" class="">Enables users to design, share, and interact with <strong data-start="559" data-end="584">blueprints of thought</strong> rather than simulations of conversation</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="626" data-end="629" class="">
<h3 data-start="631" data-end="658" class=""><strong data-start="635" data-end="658">II. Core Principles</strong></h3>
<ol data-start="660" data-end="1626">
<li data-start="660" data-end="857" class="">
<p data-start="663" data-end="857" class=""><strong data-start="663" data-end="687">Structure Over Story</strong><br data-start="687" data-end="690">
The atomic unit of meaning is a <strong data-start="725" data-end="735">schema</strong>, not a narrative. Interfaces should privilege <em data-start="782" data-end="856">concept hierarchies, semantic lattices, flowcharts, and object relations</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="859" data-end="1036" class="">
<p data-start="862" data-end="1036" class=""><strong data-start="862" data-end="903">Interpretation as Opt-In, Not Default</strong><br data-start="903" data-end="906">
All reformattings (e.g., podcastification, narrative banter, chat simulation) must be opt-in overlays‚Äînot auto-applied filters.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1038" data-end="1231" class="">
<p data-start="1041" data-end="1231" class=""><strong data-start="1041" data-end="1065">Cognitive Neutrality</strong><br data-start="1065" data-end="1068">
The system honors diverse cognition: anendophasic, aphantasic, synesthetic, verbal, non-verbal. Schema is the shared substrate; modality is layered, not forced.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1233" data-end="1439" class="">
<p data-start="1236" data-end="1439" class=""><strong data-start="1236" data-end="1260">Compression Fidelity</strong><br data-start="1260" data-end="1263">
The original compression of the author‚Äôs schema (e.g., bullet point, data table, logic gate) is considered <em data-start="1373" data-end="1388">authoritative</em>. No system-generated elaboration can overwrite it.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1441" data-end="1626" class="">
<p data-start="1444" data-end="1626" class=""><strong data-start="1444" data-end="1469">Epistemic Sovereignty</strong><br data-start="1469" data-end="1472">
Users retain full control over the form, rhythm, and visibility of their thoughts. Schemas are not auto-morphed into narrative without explicit intent.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1628" data-end="1631" class="">
<h3 data-start="1633" data-end="1672" class=""><strong data-start="1637" data-end="1672">III. Schema Objects (S-Objects)</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1674" data-end="1715" class="">Think of these as atomic cognitive units:</p>
<ul data-start="1717" data-end="2141">
<li data-start="1717" data-end="1775" class="">
<p data-start="1719" data-end="1775" class=""><strong data-start="1719" data-end="1739">Definition Block</strong> ‚Äì names a term and anchors its role</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1776" data-end="1833" class="">
<p data-start="1778" data-end="1833" class=""><strong data-start="1778" data-end="1794">Concept Node</strong> ‚Äì a labeled idea, variable, or element</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1834" data-end="1911" class="">
<p data-start="1836" data-end="1911" class=""><strong data-start="1836" data-end="1854">Affordance Map</strong> ‚Äì describes possible actions or interactions in a system</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1912" data-end="1990" class="">
<p data-start="1914" data-end="1990" class=""><strong data-start="1914" data-end="1932">Gate Condition</strong> ‚Äì constraint or conditional access point (privilege gate)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1991" data-end="2060" class="">
<p data-start="1993" data-end="2060" class=""><strong data-start="1993" data-end="2010">Feedback Loop</strong> ‚Äì dynamic modifier tracking input/output response</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2061" data-end="2141" class="">
<p data-start="2063" data-end="2141" class=""><strong data-start="2063" data-end="2079">Lattice Link</strong> ‚Äì relation between concepts (causal, conditional, analogical)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2143" data-end="2227" class="">Each S-Object is a <strong data-start="2162" data-end="2190">node in a semantic graph</strong>. No story. No arc. Just clean logic.</p>
<hr data-start="2229" data-end="2232" class="">
<h3 data-start="2234" data-end="2263" class=""><strong data-start="2238" data-end="2263">IV. Interaction Modes</strong></h3>
<ol data-start="2265" data-end="2778">
<li data-start="2265" data-end="2392" class="">
<p data-start="2268" data-end="2392" class=""><strong data-start="2268" data-end="2286">Construct Mode</strong> ‚Äì Build and navigate your schema visually or textually (like a concept graph, UML diagram, or LaTeX doc).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2393" data-end="2511" class="">
<p data-start="2396" data-end="2511" class=""><strong data-start="2396" data-end="2411">Glance Mode</strong> ‚Äì Instant high-level view of structure without summary (like skimming an API or table of contents).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2512" data-end="2615" class="">
<p data-start="2515" data-end="2615" class=""><strong data-start="2515" data-end="2531">Inspect Mode</strong> ‚Äì Zoom into any node to see metadata, linked concepts, logic flows, or constraints.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2616" data-end="2778" class="">
<p data-start="2619" data-end="2778" class=""><strong data-start="2619" data-end="2647">Simulate Mode (Optional)</strong> ‚Äì If desired, simulate the schema as an interactive system, rule engine, or even narrative‚Äîbut only <em data-start="2748" data-end="2755">after</em> structural validation.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="2780" data-end="2783" class="">
<h3 data-start="2785" data-end="2830" class=""><strong data-start="2789" data-end="2830">V. Export Layers (Controlled Filters)</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2832" data-end="2881" class="">SFIP supports explicit export formats, including:</p>
<ul data-start="2882" data-end="3120">
<li data-start="2882" data-end="2943" class="">
<p data-start="2884" data-end="2943" class=""><strong data-start="2884" data-end="2902">Narrative Mode</strong> (if you want GPT-style podcastification)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2944" data-end="2982" class="">
<p data-start="2946" data-end="2982" class=""><strong data-start="2946" data-end="2967">Presentation Mode</strong> (clean slides)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2983" data-end="3052" class="">
<p data-start="2985" data-end="3052" class=""><strong data-start="2985" data-end="3004">Code/Logic Mode</strong> (e.g., JSON schema, Python class, Prolog rules)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3053" data-end="3120" class="">
<p data-start="3055" data-end="3120" class=""><strong data-start="3055" data-end="3071">Semantic Map</strong> (interactive concept lattice or knowledge graph)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3122" data-end="3163" class="">You choose the filter. You set the terms.</p>
