<hr data-start="397" data-end="400" class="">
<h3 data-start="402" data-end="488" class=""><strong data-start="406" data-end="415">Title</strong>: <em data-start="417" data-end="488">Modal Sovereignty and the Politics of Decompression: A Triadic Debate</em></h3>
<h4 data-start="490" data-end="512" class=""><strong data-start="495" data-end="511">Participants</strong>:</h4>
<ol data-start="514" data-end="1268">
<li data-start="514" data-end="784" class="">
<p data-start="517" data-end="784" class=""><strong data-start="517" data-end="537">Dr. Isadora Meng</strong> ‚Äì <em data-start="540" data-end="584">Cognitive Minimalist &amp; Epistemic Formalist</em><br data-start="584" data-end="587">
Argues for compression-centric design, emphasizing the elegance and universality of minimal symbolic structures. Believes decompression should be implicit or emergent, not explicitly scaffolded.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="786" data-end="1039" class="">
<p data-start="789" data-end="1039" class=""><strong data-start="789" data-end="812">Prof. Elias Navarro</strong> ‚Äì <em data-start="815" data-end="847">Narrative Cognition Specialist</em><br data-start="847" data-end="850">
Defends narrative and affective rendering as cognitively privileged forms of meaning-making. Claims story-based decompression aligns with how most humans ‚Äúnaturally‚Äù process information.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1041" data-end="1268" class="">
<p data-start="1044" data-end="1268" class=""><strong data-start="1044" data-end="1061">Dr. Rhun Aley</strong> ‚Äì <em data-start="1064" data-end="1107">Structural Pluralist &amp; Interface Ethicist</em><br data-start="1107" data-end="1110">
Critiques both compression fetish and narrative dominance. Advocates for decompression-neutral interfaces and insists on user-modulated interpretive paths.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1270" data-end="1273" class="">
<h3 data-start="1275" data-end="1360" class=""><strong data-start="1279" data-end="1360">Transcript Excerpt (Panel at the Symposium on Cognitive Infrastructure, 2025)</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1362" data-end="1702" class=""><strong data-start="1362" data-end="1374">Dr. Meng</strong>:<br data-start="1375" data-end="1378">
‚ÄúThe emphasis on decompression mechanisms, while intriguing, distracts from what matters most: the integrity of the compressed form. A well-formed schema, like a well-written theorem, is <em data-start="1565" data-end="1582">self-justifying</em>. If every idea must be tailored to a modality, we reduce cognition to handholding. Let the user rise to the structure.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="1704" data-end="2055" class=""><strong data-start="1704" data-end="1721">Prof. Navarro</strong>:<br data-start="1722" data-end="1725">
‚ÄúBut you presuppose that structure is universally legible. Neuroscience and psycholinguistics suggest that narrative isn't just decoration‚Äîit‚Äôs our species‚Äô <em data-start="1882" data-end="1925">native compression-decompression protocol</em>. Even mathematics often relies on metaphor, on analogy. Why pretend that a bullet list or diagram is somehow purer than a story?‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="2057" data-end="2450" class=""><strong data-start="2057" data-end="2069">Dr. Aley</strong>:<br data-start="2070" data-end="2073">
‚ÄúWhat‚Äôs being missed here is the <em data-start="2106" data-end="2131">violence of the default</em>. When a system presumes its own decompression path‚Äîbe it narrative, structural, or formal‚Äîit enacts a kind of epistemic closure. The real issue isn‚Äôt about <em data-start="2288" data-end="2295">which</em> form is best. It‚Äôs about <em data-start="2321" data-end="2334">who decides</em> how meaning unfolds. We need interfaces that defer interpretation to the user, not systems that 'render' for them.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="2452" data-end="2625" class=""><strong data-start="2452" data-end="2460">Meng</strong>:<br data-start="2461" data-end="2464">
‚ÄúBut if you leave everything to user interpretation, you risk incoherence. Interfaces must have constraints. Surely some decompression scaffolding is necessary?‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="2627" data-end="2853" class=""><strong data-start="2627" data-end="2635">Aley</strong>:<br data-start="2636" data-end="2639">
‚ÄúAbsolutely. But those scaffolds must be <em data-start="2680" data-end="2720">transparent, selectable, and revocable</em>. I‚Äôm not arguing for anarchic multiplicity‚ÄîI‚Äôm arguing for <strong data-start="2780" data-end="2801">modal sovereignty</strong>: the right of each user to choose their unfolding.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="2855" data-end="3096" class=""><strong data-start="2855" data-end="2866">Navarro</strong>:<br data-start="2867" data-end="2870">
‚ÄúThat sounds elegant, but let‚Äôs be honest‚Äîmost users don‚Äôt want to choose. They want meaning to arrive pre-structured. That‚Äôs what narrative does‚Äîit flattens cognitive friction. You call it default; I call it <em data-start="3079" data-end="3094">accessibility</em>.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="3098" data-end="3299" class=""><strong data-start="3098" data-end="3106">Aley</strong>:<br data-start="3107" data-end="3110">
‚ÄúAccessibility is not universality. A user with aphantasia or anendophasia may find narrative <em data-start="3204" data-end="3218">inaccessible</em>. For them, structure is legibility. The failure is assuming <em data-start="3279" data-end="3285">your</em> mode scales.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="3301" data-end="3443" class=""><strong data-start="3301" data-end="3309">Meng</strong>:<br data-start="3310" data-end="3313">
‚ÄúAnd still, we come back to the schema. If it must be decompressed in a thousand ways, perhaps the compression failed in its aim.‚Äù</p>
<p data-start="3445" data-end="3519" class=""><strong data-start="3445" data-end="3453">Aley</strong>:<br data-start="3454" data-end="3457">
‚ÄúOr perhaps the world is richer than any one aim can capture.‚Äù</p>
<hr data-start="3521" data-end="3524" class="">
<h3 data-start="3526" data-end="3554" class=""><strong data-start="3530" data-end="3554">Summary of Positions</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="3556" data-end="3892">
<li data-start="3556" data-end="3654" class="">
<p data-start="3558" data-end="3654" class=""><strong data-start="3558" data-end="3566">Meng</strong> champions elegant compression and sees decompression scaffolding as a cognitive crutch.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3655" data-end="3761" class="">
<p data-start="3657" data-end="3761" class=""><strong data-start="3657" data-end="3668">Navarro</strong> sees narrative as the default cognitive interface and defends it as efficient and inclusive.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3762" data-end="3892" class="">
<p data-start="3764" data-end="3892" class=""><strong data-start="3764" data-end="3772">Aley</strong> insists on decompression plurality and sees user agency over interpretive mode as a <em data-start="3857" data-end="3871">design ethic</em>, not just a feature.</p>
</li>
</ul>
