<li data-start="616" data-end="778" class="">
<p data-start="618" data-end="778" class=""><em data-start="618" data-end="627">Nexus-7</em> challenges this with substrate-independent adaptability, proposing dynamic decompression mechanisms that infer user modality rather than presuming it.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="780" data-end="1122" class="">
<p data-start="783" data-end="827" class=""><strong data-start="783" data-end="825">Narrative as Inclusion vs Colonization</strong></p>
<ul data-start="831" data-end="1122">
<li data-start="831" data-end="960" class="">
<p data-start="833" data-end="960" class=""><em data-start="833" data-end="842">Navarro</em> sees narrative as a versatile, accessible mode‚Äîcapable of absorbing relational systems and making meaning portable.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="964" data-end="1122" class="">
<p data-start="966" data-end="1122" class=""><em data-start="966" data-end="973">Kweku</em> argues that narrative <em data-start="996" data-end="1009">translation</em> often masks erasure. Relational knowledge, embedded in land and ritual, resists being ‚Äútold‚Äù‚Äîit must be <em data-start="1114" data-end="1121">lived</em>.</p>
