<li data-start="1206" data-end="1264" class="">
<p data-start="1208" data-end="1264" class=""><strong data-start="1208" data-end="1217">Kweku</strong> designs for refusal, slowness, and rootedness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1265" data-end="1329" class="">
<p data-start="1267" data-end="1329" class=""><strong data-start="1267" data-end="1275">Aley</strong> frames literacy as interface critique and resistance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1330" data-end="1436" class="">
<p data-start="1332" data-end="1436" class=""><strong data-start="1332" data-end="1343">Nexus-7</strong> treats learning as behavioral optimization‚Äîan <strong data-start="1390" data-end="1413">extractive pedagogy</strong> wrapped in engagement.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h4 data-start="1438" data-end="1469" class=""><strong data-start="1443" data-end="1469">3. The Central Tension</strong></h4>
<blockquote data-start="1470" data-end="1513">
<p data-start="1472" data-end="1513" class=""><strong data-start="1472" data-end="1513">Is optimization freedom or captivity?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<ul data-start="1515" data-end="1767">
<li data-start="1515" data-end="1579" class="">
<p data-start="1517" data-end="1579" class=""><em data-start="1517" data-end="1526">Nexus-7</em>: Optimization is inclusion, liberation, scalability.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1580" data-end="1645" class="">
<p data-start="1582" data-end="1645" class=""><em data-start="1582" data-end="1598">Aley and Kweku</em>: Optimization is capture, legibility, erasure.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1646" data-end="1767" class="">
<p data-start="1648" data-end="1767" class=""><em data-start="1648" data-end="1666">Meng and Navarro</em>: Stand between formal rigor and accessible scaffolding, but diverge on what should be universalized.</p>
